    "knowledge_point": "Calculating the x-Intercepts of an Ellipse By Completing the Square",
    "problem": "Calculate the points where the ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+4x+54y+76=0$ intercepts the $x$-axis.",
    "worked_solution": "To calculate the $x$-intercepts, we need to substitute $y=0$ into the equation of the ellipse and solve the resulting equation for $x.$ We do this and solve the resulting equation by completing the square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}+4x+54y+76 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+9\\cdot {0}^{2}+4x+54\\cdot 0+76 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2}+4x+76 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}-4+76 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2}+72 & =0 \\\\ (x+2)^{2} & =-72.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $(x+2)^{2}\\ge 0$, there is no $x$ such that $(x+2)^{2}=-72.$ Therefore, the ellipse has no $x$-intercepts, meaning that it does not intercept the $x$-axis at any point.",
